An unbounded synchronization point, expressed directly in the language:

```go
func producer(ch chan<- int) {
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        ch <- i           // blocks until a receiver is ready
    }
    close(ch)
}

func consumer(ch <-chan int) {
    for v := range ch {  // blocks until a value arrives, exits on close
        fmt.Println(v)
    }
}

func main() {
    ch := make(chan int)   // unbuffered channel
    go producer(ch)
    consumer(ch)
}
```

No shared variable exists between `producer` and `consumer` at all — the channel `ch` *is* the entire communication surface. The unbuffered channel forces a **synchronous handoff**: `producer`'s send blocks until `consumer` is actually ready to receive that specific value, which is a strong ordering guarantee with no separate lock needed.

"Use goroutines and channels to fan out work to N workers and collect results" has a canonical shape worth knowing cold:

```go
jobs := make(chan int, 100)
results := make(chan int, 100)

for w := 0; w < N; w++ {
    go func() {
        for j := range jobs {       // each worker pulls from the shared jobs channel
            results <- process(j)
        }
    }()
}

for _, j := range allJobs { jobs <- j }
close(jobs)

for i := 0; i < len(allJobs); i++ { fmt.Println(<-results) }
```

N worker goroutines all read from the *same* `jobs` channel — Go automatically distributes each job to whichever worker happens to be free, with no explicit load-balancing code needed, because a channel with multiple readers naturally does round-robin-ish distribution as a side effect of blocking receive semantics. `close(jobs)` after sending is what lets each worker's `range` loop terminate cleanly instead of blocking forever — skip it, and you've built exactly the goroutine leak the previous slide warned about.
